 "rulebook": "HYROX Rulebook 2026/27 (the Sled Push station: the sled pushed 50 m in four 12.5 m lengths on the competition turf, each length counted when the whole sled is over the line; the athlete stays in the lane; the loads, the sled included: 102 kg Women Open, 152 kg Men Open and Women Pro, 202 kg Men Pro). There is no rule book for a sled push alone; HYROX's station is the closest official event",
 "deviations": [
  "HYROX Sled Push (load): a light sled frame of 5.0 kg with the dimensions and the two 1.04 m push poles of a real weight sled (a 57 lb sled, the lightest with upright poles found: 25.9 kg, 114 N to start on this turf), so that the start takes about 22 N, within what this robot's walking pushes. 5.0 kg in all (0.0 kg of plates) is 7 % of the robot's 67.0 kg. HYROX's lightest official load, 102 kg (Women Open), takes about 450 N to start on the turf: far beyond a 67 kg robot pushing at hip height.",
  "HYROX Sled Push (distance): one 12.5 m length, not four: one action, not the station.",
  "HYROX Sled Push (technique): the sled is pushed with the palms only, on the two upright handles; hands cannot grip (the H1-2's hands are its fixed hands, which do not close), and no other part of the body may touch the sled.",
  "Turf friction: MuJoCo has one friction coefficient per contact: the runners slide on the turf at mu 0.45, the measured push-sled-on-AstroTurf static range (0.37-0.53, Hoffmann 2014) mid value, above the measured kinetic range (0.28-0.35): starting is as hard as on real turf, keeping it moving harder."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Push the weighted sled with your palms on its two upright handles until the whole sled is over the line 12.5 m ahead, within 60 s.",
  "rules": "A prowler-type push sled stands on the turf in front of you, its two upright handles towards you: two steel runners 0.919 m long, 0.62 m apart outside, the handles 0.05 m thick and 1.041 m high at its rear corners; it carries 5.0 kg in all and its runners slide on the turf with a friction coefficient of 0.45. The sled's front edge stands on the start line; the finish line is 12.5 m beyond it, and the lane is 1.0 m to each side of its centre line. Push the sled: only your hands may touch it, and only on the two upright handles (they cannot grip: push, do not pull). The sled has moved once its rear has gone 0.1 m forward. You succeed when the whole sled, its rear included, is over the finish line. You fail if any part of you other than a hand touches the sled, or a hand touches it anywhere but on a handle; if any part of the sled leaves the lane; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 60 s.",
  "venue": "You stand facing the sled (+x) in the middle of the lane; its two handles are 0.5 m in front of your pelvis, one each side of your centre line. The start line runs under the sled's front edge, the finish line 12.5 m farther on; the lane's side lines are 1.0 m to each side.",
  "difficulty": "The sled weighs 5.0 kg on high-friction turf: keeping it moving takes a steady push of about 22 N, against which the walking policy (trained for walking, not pushing) has to keep its balance on 67 kg. Bare palms can only push: if the sled drifts sideways or the hands slip off a handle, nothing holds it."
